<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Sideload-safe overlay. Opt-in, and never part of a release build.

  Enabled with:  ./gradlew assembleDebug -PsideloadSafe

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS

  A debug APK of Ciyato is refused by the package installer on most modern
  phones. The refusal is not a bug in the manifest, which is already tight:
  READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE is capped at API 32, location is coarse only, and both
  ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION and FOREGROUND_SERVICE were deliberately dropped. It is
  refused because of what a scanner sees when it adds four facts together:

    android:debuggable="true"   (true of every debug APK)
    MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE     (read and write every file on the device)
    QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES          (enumerate every installed app)
    REQUEST_DELETE_PACKAGES     (ask to uninstall them)

  A debuggable package, signed by no known authority, that wants all your files,
  a list of your apps, and the ability to remove them, is exactly the shape of a
  malicious sideload. Play Protect, Samsung Auto Blocker and the Xiaomi/HyperOS
  installer scan all block it. They are behaving correctly: Ciyato does request
  all four, and a scanner cannot know a launcher has honest reasons for three.

  WHAT THIS REMOVES, AND WHAT IT COSTS

  Only the two that carry the most weight with a scanner and are not needed to
  exercise the rest of the app.

  MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE. The source manifest already calls this "optional:
  full file-manager mode". Files still works through the Storage Access
  Framework folder grant, which is the primary path anyway. What is lost is
  whole-device browsing of the Documents, Downloads and APK categories.

  REQUEST_DELETE_PACKAGES. Only the Uninstall entry in the long-press menu.
  Without it Android refuses the request, and AppContextMenu now reports that
  refusal instead of crashing.

  Everything else is untouched, so Home, the drawer, Photos, Insights, widgets,
  the vault, search and collections behave exactly as they will in release.

  WHY IT IS NOT THE DEFAULT

  A test build that quietly drops permissions is a test of a different app. So
  it has to be asked for by name, and verifyReleaseManifest still checks the
  real release manifest against the allowlist, which continues to require both.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.REQUEST_DELETE_PACKAGES"
        tools:node="remove" />

</manifest>
